// A `ref` binding taken from a plain LOCAL aliases that local (§M.13.2,
// ERRATA E86, and the worked example in JUX-POINTERS-REFERENCES-GUIDE §2.2).
// The two names are one object from then on, so every write through either is
// seen by both.
//
// Java has no `ref`, so its twin models the same object with a one-element
// array, which Java shares the way Jux shares a `ref` cell. Both programs do
// the same thing and must print the same numbers, which is the point: before
// the fix Jux printed 0 / 5 / 5 where the guide, and Java, say 5 / 5 / 9.

int aliasParam(int seed) {
    ref int r = seed;
    r = seed + 1;
    return seed;
}

public void main() {
    int total = 0;
    ref int acc = total;
    acc = 5;
    print(total);
    print(acc);
    total = 9;
    print(acc);

    // A String is a value type too, so the same rule applies to it.
    String label = "one";
    ref String labelAlias = label;
    labelAlias = "two";
    print(label);
    print(labelAlias);

    // A ref from a literal has nothing to alias: it gets a fresh object.
    ref int fresh = 3;
    fresh = 4;
    print(fresh);

    // A parameter is a variable of its body, so a ref aliases it there.
    print(aliasParam(5));
    int caller = 5;
    print(aliasParam(caller));
    print(caller);
}
